The page table is left untouched until a page is used.

gc_allocate_ptes callocs the page table -- 16 bytes for every GC page of the
dynamic space, 2 MB at a nodecode image's 8 GB -- trusting, in its own words,
that "the C library typically implements this efficiently with mmap()". glibc
does; macOS's medium allocator (blocks up to 8 MB) zeroes a reused block
itself, so the whole table was resident while the heap used a few thousand
entries: the 2 MB of MALLOC_MEDIUM a macOS gateway and shell both carried
(2026-09-25, lane run 36190378614). On Darwin the table, the pin table and
page_execp come from os_allocate: fresh pages read zero, which is what calloc
promised, and stay untouched until an entry is written.

--- a/src/runtime/coreparse.c	2026-09-25 13:44:22.672881717 -0700
+++ b/src/runtime/coreparse.c	2026-09-25 14:23:15.888833343 -0700
@@ -1134,15 +1134,24 @@
      *   index at 1+ appears to start a contiguous block even though
      *   it corresponds to no page)
      */
-    page_table = calloc(page_table_pages+2, sizeof(struct page));
+#ifdef LISP_FEATURE_DARWIN
+    /* Not calloc on Darwin: its medium allocator zeroes a reused block
+     * itself, so the table -- 2 MB for an 8 GB space, of which the heap uses
+     * a few thousand entries -- was resident whole. Fresh pages read zero. */
+#define ZERO_TABLE(n, size) (void*)os_allocate(ALIGN_UP((n)*(size), os_vm_page_size))
+#else
+#define ZERO_TABLE(n, size) calloc(n, size)
+#endif
+    page_table = ZERO_TABLE(page_table_pages+2, sizeof(struct page));
     gc_assert(page_table);
     page_table[0].gen = 9; // an arbitrary never-used value
     ++page_table;
-    gc_page_pins = calloc(page_table_pages, 1);
+    gc_page_pins = ZERO_TABLE(page_table_pages, 1);
     gc_assert(gc_page_pins);
 #ifdef LISP_FEATURE_DARWIN_JIT
-    page_execp = calloc(page_table_pages, 1);
+    page_execp = ZERO_TABLE(page_table_pages, 1);
 #endif
+#undef ZERO_TABLE
 
     long num_gc_cards = 1 + gc_card_table_mask;
 #ifdef LISP_FEATURE_X86_64
